diff --git a/docs/internationalization.md b/docs/internationalization.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3450eb8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/internationalization.md
@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+# Internationalization
+
+Fizzy supports English (`en`, the default) and Japanese (`ja`). Append
+`?locale=ja` or `?locale=en` to any page to choose a language. Unsupported
+locale values are ignored. The selection is kept in the browser session and,
+after authentication, on the global `Identity`, so it follows a person across
+accounts and future sign-ins. Deployments must run `bin/rails db:migrate`.
+
+## Adding or changing text
+
+* Put readable application text in `config/locales`, including page titles,
+  accessibility attributes, confirmation messages, image captions, native
+  bridge labels, and CSS-generated text. Keep English wording and punctuation
+  unchanged when extracting an existing message.
+* Use `t` in templates and `I18n.t` in Ruby. Use interpolation for dynamic
+  values, and count translations instead of English `pluralize`.
+* Constants should hold translation **keys**, not the result of `I18n.t`.
+  Translate at rendering time. Do not translate identifiers, URLs, or
+  user-authored names and content.
+* Use `I18n.l` for readable server-side dates. Date formats live in
+  `en.formats.yml` and `ja.rails.yml`. Machine timestamps and URL date parameters
+  remain locale independent. Japanese uses a single plural form (`other`).
+* Client-side messages live under `javascript` and are embedded in the page's
+  `i18n` meta tag. Use `helpers/i18n` from JavaScript. The selected document
+  language, not the browser's preferred language, controls date formatting.
+* The rich-text editor package's own JavaScript strings are intentionally
+  untouched. Fizzy's surrounding forms and prompts are translated normally.
+
+## Locale boundaries
+
+The authentication concern includes `Localization`, which wraps each request
+in `I18n.with_locale`. HTML fragment/collection cache keys and ETags include the
+locale. Public board HTML is privately cached because it can depend on a
+session's language. A tracked locale meta tag forces a full Turbo reload on a
+language change; the offline cache is cleared as well.
+
+Mailers resolve the recipient identity's locale while evaluating both subjects
+and templates. An anonymous sign-in can pass its session choice to the magic
+link email without modifying an unauthenticated identity. Queued notification
+and pin broadcasts resolve the recipient's current preference at delivery time.
+System comments are stored in their original English form and rendered in the
+viewer's language from the corresponding event. User-written comments are not
+translated.
+
+Static error pages cannot know the viewer's preference. They contain both
+English and Japanese, with language attributes on the Japanese sections.
+
+## Verification
+
+```
+bin/rails test test/controllers/localization_test.rb \
+  test/models/localization_test.rb test/mailers/localization_test.rb \
+  test/jobs/localized_broadcast_job_test.rb
+bin/rails test:system
+```
+
+The catalog test checks coverage and interpolation parity. The request and mail
+checks exercise persistence, invalid values, cache isolation, public pages,
+Japanese dates/counts, and recipient-specific delivery. The browser test checks
+Turbo language changes and client-rendered relative times.
